\section{Diagnostic Innovations, Phase Geometry, and Observational Validation}
\label{sec:diagnostics_and_validation}

\subsection{Visual Diagnostics: Fast--Slow Geometry and Trajectory Pathways}
\label{subsec:visual_phase_space}

The coupled GSPT-SBL system traverses three distinct operational regimes in the reduced phase space $(q, \Delta)$, as illustrated in the geometric phase portraits:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Evening Run-Up (Active Sheet):} Strong surface shear maintains positive net forcing ($\Delta > 0$), confining the state vector to the active attracting sheet $\mathcal{S}_0^{\mathrm{act}}$ where $q = q_+(\Delta)$.
    \item \textbf{Sudden Collapse (Fold Catastrophe):} Sustained radiative cooling shifts forcing below the saddle-node turning point $\Delta_{\mathrm{fold}} = -\frac{\beta^2}{4}$. Loss of normal hyperbolicity forces a fast vertical collapse on an $\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{-1})$ timescale down to the laminar sheet.
    \item \textbf{Re-Ignition Recovery (Transcritical Point):} As decoupled geostrophic wind aloft accelerates and shear re-accumulates, forcing increases back toward $\Delta = 0$. At this transcritical bifurcation, the laminar boundary branch becomes repelling, enabling deterministic turbulent re-ignition without ad-hoc empirical switching functions.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Tri-Height Observability and Emergent Interfaces}
\label{subsec:tri_height_observability}

Traditional boundary-layer parameterizations rely on static, prescribed layer heights that fail to capture dynamic decoupling. To link fast--slow manifold geometry directly to observational field metrics, we introduce a novel \textbf{Tri-Height Diagnostic Framework} comprising three physically observable scale heights:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Diffusivity Cutoff Height ($h_D$):} The altitude where vertical eddy diffusivity drops below subgrid background levels ($K_m \le K_{\mathrm{bg}}$), capturing the immediate loss of vertical turbulent transport.
    \item \textbf{TKE Energy Floor Height ($h_e$):} The height where turbulent kinetic energy decays to the background floor ($e \le \delta$), representing the physical boundary of active turbulent mixing.
    \item \textbf{TKE Gradient Height ($h_{\partial e}$):} The altitude of maximum structural vertical gradient ($|\partial e / \partial z| = \max$), pinpointing the internal shear-stratification interface.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}[Triple-Point Convergence as an Emergent Interface]
In uncoupled or poorly parameterized models, $h_D$, $h_e$, and $h_{\partial e}$ diverge significantly across the vertical column. Under the GSPT manifold framework, tracking these three heights over a $12$-hour nocturnal cycle reveals an explicit \textbf{triple-point convergence} ($h_D \approx h_e \approx h_{\partial e}$) as the system approaches collapse. This convergence provides definitive physical and observational evidence of an \textbf{emergent sharp geometric interface}—the dynamic capping inversion boundary.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Adaptive Scale Height $h_{\mathrm{eff}}$ Versus Runaway Cooling}
\label{subsec:adaptive_scale_height}

To close vertical column momentum without prescribing fixed SBL depths, the framework defines a dynamic, state-dependent scale height:
\begin{equation}
h_{\mathrm{eff}}(t) = \frac{\int_0^H z\,e(z,t)\,\mathrm{d}z}{\int_0^H e(z,t)\,\mathrm{d}z}.
\label{eq:h_eff_definition}
\end{equation}
Traditional static height prescriptions enforce rigid mixing lengths aloft during stable transitions, severely suppressing turbulence and inducing unphysical "runaway surface cooling" \citep{Derbyshire1990, Holtslag1993}. In contrast, $h_{\mathrm{eff}}$ adapts dynamically to the state vector: as surface turbulence quenches, $h_{\mathrm{eff}}$ shrinks rapidly near the ground while preserving realistic residual mixing lengths aloft. This dynamic adaptation prevents runaway cooling while maintaining the shear capacity needed to drive subsequent inertial re-ignition.

\subsection{Field Campaign Validation and the "RMSE Paradox"}
\label{subsec:rmse_paradox}

The model was validated against high-resolution field observations from CASES-99 \citep{poulos2002cases99}, FLOSS, and SHEBA. Comparative evaluation reveals a counter-intuitive outcome termed the \textbf{Geometric RMSE Paradox}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{High-Roughness Regimes (FLOSS / Canopy):} Yield remarkably low Root-Mean-Square Error (RMSE) fit values against standard 1D column trajectories.
    \item \textbf{Low-Roughness Regimes (CASES-99 / Open Prairie):} Produce higher RMSE metrics despite capturing the underlying physics with greater fidelity.
\end{itemize}

\begin{remark}[Frictional Regularization Framing]
Rather than indicating model failure in smooth terrain, this paradox reflects a fundamental geometric mechanism: high surface roughness (FLOSS) acts as a strong \textbf{dynamic frictional damper}, pinning state-space trajectories tightly to the invariant manifold $\mathcal{S}_\varepsilon$. Conversely, smooth open terrain (CASES-99) minimizes surface damping, allowing trajectories to execute unconstrained global dynamics—including wide, large-amplitude Blackadar inertial loops in $(U,V)$ space. Standard point-wise RMSE metrics penalize these unconstrained non-linear state-space excursions, mistaking rich dynamic trajectory exploration for model error.
\end{remark}

\subsection{24-Hour Diurnal Dynamics and Convective Preconditioning}
\label{subsec:diurnal_narrative}

Extending the nocturnal GSPT analysis across a full $24$-hour diurnal cycle—incorporating observational context from extreme nocturnal regimes such as the Taklimakan Desert—highlights \citep{Su2024,Zhang2024Taklimakan} the global closure of boundary-layer transitions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Nocturnal Coupling:} Nighttime surface cooling initiates fast fold-mediated turbulence collapse, decoupling the upper boundary layer from surface friction.
    \item \textbf{Inertial Acceleration:} Decoupling triggers an unconstrained Blackadar inertial oscillation, forming a supergeostrophic Low-Level Jet (LLJ) nose aloft ($z \approx 200\text{--}300\text{ m}$).
    \item \textbf{Convective Preconditioning:} The nocturnal inversion cap and elevated LLJ shear store significant kinetic and thermal potential. At morning solar onset, this residual nocturnal shear structure provides the essential structural prerequisites for rapid morning boundary-layer growth, accelerating the breakout transition into the deep daytime Convective Boundary Layer (CBL).
\end{enumerate}